1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
It's mathematically necessary that bond prices and interest rates move in opposite directions.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,040
So again, one of the most commonly misunderstood things in the world.

3
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:13,320
People, once people really know it, then they act like no one could ever be confused about

4
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:14,320
it.

5
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:15,880
It's really easy to be confused about this.

6
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:16,880
Right?

7
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:20,360
I'll even go so far as to say it's counterintuitive.

8
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:22,080
So same idea here.

9
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:25,340
This here is looking at the price.

10
00:00:25,340 --> 00:00:27,620
This here is looking at the rate of return.

11
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:32,520
So it makes sense that when the price, that if risk is bringing the price down, it's bringing

12
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:33,520
the rate of return up.

13
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:34,520
You all feel good about that?

14
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:35,520
Same thing here.

15
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:36,520
Notice, that price could have gone up because the riskiness changed.

16
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:37,520
So me saying this, these are not two different things.

17
00:00:37,520 --> 00:00:39,600
These could be the numbers that reflect exactly this.

18
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:57,160
So it could be that riskiness goes down and then the price of the asset goes from 90 to

19
00:00:57,160 --> 00:00:58,160
95.

20
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:00,880
So that means the risk just steps lower.

21
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:04,720
And then when that happens, then we're going from a normal return.

22
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:05,720
So let's see.

23
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:06,720
So yeah.

24
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:07,720
So let's see.

25
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:08,720
Yes.

26
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:11,720
And then because it's less risky, then that means that we are getting a smaller risk premium.

27
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:13,560
We're getting a smaller risk premium.

28
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:17,760
So then that means the rate of return will be lower.

29
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:18,760
Let's see.

30
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:21,280
I'm getting some confused faces here.

31
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:22,280
Let's see.

32
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:23,280
You anyway.

33
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:28,000
So just to repeat, bond is a face value of 100.

34
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,240
When it sells for 90, what is the interest rate that you're earning on that bond?

35
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:33,240
11%.

36
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:34,240
Roughly 11%.

37
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:40,720
And then suppose that it turns out there's new information, like the socialists lose

38
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:44,240
power so there's not going to be a default on the bonds.

39
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:49,640
In this case, it makes sense the price goes up to $95 because the bond is no longer as

40
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:50,640
risky.

41
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:51,640
That means there's a lower risk discount.

42
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:56,640
But at the same time, just as a matter of pure math, this means that the interest rate

43
00:01:56,640 --> 00:01:59,840
has fallen because the price went up.

44
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:05,440
So you now earn less per year holding onto this asset because there's a smaller gap between

45
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:09,040
the current price and the maturity price.

46
00:02:09,040 --> 00:02:11,360
All right.

47
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:17,080
Now, this whole thing, though, is complicated by another fact, which is that there are many

48
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:18,080
risky assets in the world.

49
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:22,640
So this would all be fine in a world where there's just one safe asset and one risky

50
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:23,640
asset.

51
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:25,640
But in the real world, how many assets are there again?

52
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:27,640
Like is it like three, four?

53
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:28,640
We've got seven.

54
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:29,640
Yeah, there's millions of assets.

55
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:30,640
There's millions of assets.

56
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:31,640
You own billions, really.

57
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:32,640
Like every house is an asset, right?

58
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:33,640
So a huge number of assets.

59
00:02:33,640 --> 00:02:34,640
All right.

60
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:35,640
Okay.

61
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:47,960
And we go back to basic probability tells us that the average riskiness of a bundle

62
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:53,680
of different risks is going to be less than the average riskiness of the same dollar value

63
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:55,800
of a single risk.

64
00:02:55,800 --> 00:03:01,360
So the average riskiness of only owning Microsoft stock, of only owning $1,000 of Microsoft

65
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:06,600
stock, is going to be higher than the riskiness of having $1 of 1,000 different stocks, $1

66
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:14,480
each of 1,000 different stocks, because those stocks do not all move in perfect synchronicity.

67
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:18,000
Whereas Microsoft stock all does move in perfect synchronicity.

68
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:19,000
It's all the same.

69
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
There might be a few different kinds of Microsoft stock, but you get the idea.

70
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:24,000
All right.

71
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,920
And what this means is that to some extent, you can diversify away risk.

72
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:31,920
So you can diversify it away.

73
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:37,920
So you can take a bunch of assets, every single one of which is risky, but then you get a

74
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:43,600
small amount of each of them, put them all together, and that bundle may not be very

75
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:44,600
risky at all.

76
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:45,600
All right.

77
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:50,800
And suppose you're a highly risk-averse agent, but then you're given this very safe bundle.

78
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:54,720
How do you feel about this very safe bundle as a risk-averse agent?

79
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:55,720
Pretty good.

80
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:59,360
It's like, well, it doesn't scare me very much, because it's $1 each of 1,000 different

81
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:00,360
things.

82
00:04:00,360 --> 00:04:06,120
So what this means is that there's a pretty simple thing that you can do to make a risk-averse

83
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:09,080
person feel safe.

84
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:12,640
And once they feel safe, you don't need to go and pay much of a premium to get the middle

85
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:13,640
of the asset.

86
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:19,360
So the result of this, then, or the general implication, is you shouldn't expect this

87
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:22,000
diversifiable risk to earn a premium.

88
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,520
So when we say that there's a risk premium that you earn, or a great return, or there's

89
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:32,120
a risk discount of the price, that doesn't refer to this diversifiable risk, where you

90
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:36,560
can basically do a simple strategy and get away from it.

91
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:40,640
This would be like, suppose that there were a simple hat that you could wear that would

92
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:44,880
completely protect you against getting hurt on a construction site.

93
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:48,320
In that case, how much would you have to pay someone who was worried about having being

94
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:49,320
crushed?

95
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:50,320
You wouldn't have to pay them anything extra.

96
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:51,320
It's like, look, here's the hat.

97
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:52,320
Solves the problem.

98
00:04:52,320 --> 00:04:53,320
It's like, OK, well, I'm really scared of having my head crushed, but given that the

99
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:54,320
hat prevents my head from being crushed, you don't have to pay me anything extra to be

100
00:04:54,320 --> 00:04:55,320
here.

101
00:04:55,320 --> 00:04:56,320
So you only would need to pay that person for the risk of the accident that the hat

102
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:57,320
doesn't protect him from.

103
00:04:57,320 --> 00:04:58,320
And similarly, you only need to go and pay this risk premium, and you don't need to pay

104
00:04:58,320 --> 00:04:59,320
the person who's worried about having their head crushed.

105
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:00,320
So you don't have to pay them anything extra to be here.

106
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:01,320
So you only would need to pay that person for the risk of the accident that the hat

107
00:05:01,320 --> 00:05:20,760
doesn't protect him from.

108
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:27,520
So when you earn a premium for what's called undiversifiable risk.

109
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:28,520
And what does this mean?

110
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:36,400
Well, if the return on an asset positively correlates with the average market rate return.

111
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:39,800
So if the asset positively correlates with the average market rate return, then you should

112
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:40,800
expect a risk premium.

113
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:44,480
Because the average market rate return, that's what you can get.

114
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:45,480
That's what you get for diversification.

115
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:51,280
But in the real world, the problem is that even if you do just have $1 each in 1,000

116
00:05:51,280 --> 00:05:55,600
different stocks, you still don't have real certainty, because the whole market sometimes

117
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:56,600
moves.

118
00:05:56,600 --> 00:05:59,360
In fact, every day, the whole market moves.

119
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:03,360
Right now, you're still unlikely to be wiped out if you have $1 each in 1,000 different

120
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:07,440
stocks, whereas you might really be wiped out if you put all $1,000 in one stock.

121
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:14,760
So it's not as risky, but still, it's not anything like a perfect solution either.

122
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:17,800
Now the further this means that if you could find something that actually had a negative

123
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:24,360
correlation with the market interest rate, then it would actually pay more than its present

124
00:06:24,360 --> 00:06:27,800
kind of value.

125
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:31,120
I don't think this actually works, but you might think of something like resume services.

126
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:35,320
It's like resume services you might think would negatively correlate with the market

127
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:36,320
rate.

128
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:37,320
I don't think it's actually true.

129
00:06:37,320 --> 00:06:43,400
I think it was the first big market crash in 2007.

130
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:47,360
In the entire S&P 500, there was one stock that went up that day.

131
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:51,160
$499 went down, one went up.

132
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:55,600
You won't be able to guess it, but it will make perfect sense when you hear it.

133
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:58,560
The one stock that went up plus Campbell's Soup.

134
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:01,840
So Campbell's Soup went up that day.

135
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:05,160
Now I have no evidence this happens generally.

136
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:07,960
It's just a very memorable example.

137
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:13,560
But the story that it's almost impossible not to tell is people say, well, there's going

138
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:17,160
to be a lot of people who are going to be cutting back on luxury foods and eating Campbell's

139
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:18,160
Soup.

140
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:23,160
So Campbell's Soup may actually increase its production during this period, possibly.

141
00:07:23,160 --> 00:07:24,160
All right.

142
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:25,160
Questions?

143
00:07:25,160 --> 00:07:26,160
Questions?

144
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:27,160
All right.

145
00:07:27,160 --> 00:07:28,160
So these are some basics.

146
00:07:28,160 --> 00:07:33,160
So I just did my talk with Nassim Taleb.

